# 1. Native operation authority owns coordination

Date: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict
and implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D008,D009,D013,D014,D016,D019.

V1 already has deep domain/request modules, but GPUI callers still coordinate
slots, protected-key IO and ordered effects. React must not inherit that authority.

Choose a principal-bound Rust actor with dispatch(Intent) and observe(ViewScope).
Keep feature transitions domain-owned inside one initial core crate. Native
bootstrap injects adapters and creates operator/Harness handles; JSON cannot
select the caller principal. Finite domain instructions exclude arbitrary SDK
method strings and any Harness Apply capability.

The interface includes atomic admission/context retirement, per-source supersession,
separate read/review/write/stream domains, priority/deadline/rate-limit semantics,
bounded queues, bounded delivery-sequence deduplication with safe session rotation,
single-use settlement and truthful Remote outcome. No remote IO
or blocking vault call occurs while the authoritative transition is locked.
Admitted work runs on the host's one async runtime; native adapters execute effects.

React owns raw editor text/undo. Rust owns immutable captured drafts/reviews.
Review/Apply/Save/Send capture fresh input in their actual production handlers;
Apply compares fresh input with the stored reviewed operation and executes only
that stored operation. Invalid/lossy input, changed context/draft/resource or a
consumed review refuses dispatch. Never rely only on a debounced revision.
RequestApply uses a native operator confirmation; actual Apply completion is
private, single-use and revalidated after the modal editor/context lease.
Every scoped intent/observation carries native-issued context and principal
admission. Async vault completions are generation/lease-bound; obsolete writes
receive orphan cleanup rather than reactivating credentials. Explicit one-time
secret reveal/copy stays in a scoped transient native dialog, never snapshots.

Alternatives considered under the design-it-twice skill:

| Design | Depth/locality | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| Actor dispatch/observe | Callers learn one authority seam; context and settlement stay together | Finite unions and observation backpressure need discipline |
| Typed scoped handles | Strong domain methods and revoked scopes; flexible internal composition | More handles/methods and IPC registries for callers to coordinate |
| Task-oriented scan board | Trivial first scan workflow | Public task surface grows across all parity features |

Recommend the actor with domain-specific types and a small generated presentation
facade. Retain the alternatives' explicit scoped validation and effortless scan
usage internally. Do not combine all their public surfaces or add pass-through
modules merely to obtain two method names.

Two real execution adapters (SDK/scripted), storage adapters (Keychain/session/
fault-injectable) and clocks justify internal seams. The interface is the test
surface: switch/replace/cancel while queued/dispatched, old/duplicate reply,
fresh-input races, double Apply, denied Harness Apply, unknown outcome/no replay,
priority/deadline/rate-limit containment and observation resynchronization.

See [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md) for ownership/wire details.
No app code has been written and no native/SDK test is claimed by this ADR.
# 3. Retain native Harness and own scoped identity in Rust

Date: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict
and implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D010,D011,D014–D016,D021–D023.

Desktop-server is already a Rust engine. V1 host workers and its package manifest
still require Node/managed CLI. Preserve engine depth and replace those host
requirements, rather than rewrite the agent. Source inspection alone does not
establish that a newly sealed installed bundle works without the old resources.

Native lifecycle owns sealed executable selection, bounded stdin bootstrap,
protocol supervision, process cleanup, scoped grants and protected history.
React only presents lifecycle state. Grant/tenant/project changes retire the
run; approvals bind actual call items. Preserve existing project/process/tool
restrictions, Gateway-only inference/MCP and no engine Apply.

Identity shares native discovery/HTTP/validation/storage mechanics while retaining
Academy learner, Harness inference and MCP grant policies. They are distinct
identities with distinct sign-out/refresh/context effects. Use public-client
PKCE/device flows and a no-redirect native transport; never embed a client secret
or trust renderer-selected issuer/token destinations.

Host signature/JWKS validation is a deliberate stricter v2 requirement; v1
omitted it. Validate ID/access token types according to their actual contracts,
not by decoding arbitrary JWT claims. Rotation/expiry/sleep/revocation fail closed.
Same-identity token refresh restarts the one-shot-bootstrap engine and restores
compatible in-session history; it re-confirms workspace-write permission and
never replays remote/tool effects. Declining workspace-write confirmation keeps
the draft/cancels Send without restart. Any changed ContextBinding (including
local access/model/configuration/MCP inventory) starts a new conversation; its
old history is inspect-only under the new binding. MCP-only token rotation also
rebootstraps/restores an identical binding, but retains working v1's current
project-access confirmation without an SSO refresh or another write prompt.
Native scope additionally binds per-server MCP grant IDs/catalog revisions absent
from engine equality; re-sign-in/sign-out/removal/revision change retires the
conversation. Expiry alone leaves inference usable with that non-required server
unavailable and native calls denied. This continuity is distinct from inspect-only
imported history. No token or refresh credential enters React, diagnostics, process arguments or
inherited environment. One-time operator-entered inputs remain transient.

Use independent v2 bundle/data/Keychain namespaces. Protected work is encrypted
and account/context-scoped; no plaintext fallback. Reviewed import creates a new
v2 data generation, never modifies v1, transfers no credentials/approvals and
retains a restartable receipt/rollback generation. Historical conversations are
inspect-only until fresh-context compatibility is qualified.

Tests cross native process and identity interfaces: malformed/oversized bootstrap,
queue pressure, wrong context, crash/EOF/graceful and forced cleanup, signature/
issuer/audience/key-rotation rejection, scoped sign-out, protected histories and
Node-free installed inventory. Native/live/final signed-byte proof remain separate.
See [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md) for identity/crypto proposals.
# Prisma AIRS Desktop v2 implementation plan

Status: product interview decisions D001–D023 accepted; D024/D025 settle source
terms and all four architecture candidates; D026 makes Codex the independent
chunk and phase reviewer. G00/G01 passed; implementation proceeds from C01. A passing plan review is not a passing architecture,
SDK, application, live-tenant, or package review.

## Outcome and exclusions

Deliver a fresh macOS Apple Silicon desktop application with React presentation
in Tauri, a focused Rust application core, direct Rust SCM SDK integration, and
the native Rust Harness engine. Academy supplies shared presentation and remains
the authority for remote learning. Preserve agreed working v1 behavior, using
v1 as requirements reference. Do not translate its GPUI implementation wholesale.

No desktop billing, media-generation backend, local MySQL/Redis server stack,
Node/Bun backend runtime, TypeScript workers, speculative browser client, or
in-app updater is included. Academy gamification remains inside Academy. Gateway
usage/cost/budget/limits remain operational product functionality. Build-time
JavaScript tooling and React WebView JavaScript are expected.

Source destination: `ssh://git@git-ssh.cdot.io/cdot/prisma-airs-desktop-v2.git`.
Checkout: `/Users/cdot/development/redtail/prisma-airs-desktop-v2`.

## Architectural guiding principles

1. Design deep modules: substantial behavior behind a small domain interface.
   Apply the deletion test; do not wrap every SDK method in a pass-through module.
2. The interface is the test surface. Exercise production transitions and
   adapters; use independent wire/protocol expectations for external contracts.
3. One adapter is a hypothetical seam; two real adapters justify a seam. Shared
   Academy presentation has real web/desktop consumers. Do not build speculative
   browser/backend/plugin architectures for this desktop delivery.
4. Rust owns authority and locality for tenant/credential/workspace context,
   request lifecycle, configuration review, protected storage, and approvals.
   React owns presentation and editable input, not permission to execute.
5. The SDK owns its declared transport/authentication contracts. The application
   owns interactive identity, OS storage, context admission, and workflow policy.
   Do not duplicate SDK retry/pagination/OAuth policy without a documented need.
6. Unknown outcomes and partial/stale/denied/unsupported data remain distinct.
   Never map an unknown verdict to allow or a cancelled write to successful rollback.
7. Host credentials and capabilities are explicit. No ambient environment-file
   discovery, inherited production fallback, generic renderer secret access,
   unrestricted native plugin grants, or cross-origin credential forwarding.
8. Shared appearance does not imply shared learner state. Tenant operations,
   Academy identity/progression, Harness inference identity, and MCP grants stay
   separately scoped. A tenant switch does not sign the learner out of Academy.
9. Keep provenance and reproducibility local to dependency selection. Exact
   versions, source hashes, build inputs, notices, and protocol pins are evidence;
   a neighboring active worktree is not a release dependency.
10. Measure native usability/performance and the actual installed package. A
    successful browser render or mocked IPC assertion is insufficient evidence
    for Tauri host behavior, VoiceOver, Keychain, sidecar isolation, or notarization.

Concrete interfaces, crate decomposition, shared package extraction, and storage
formats are outputs of G01. This plan defines behavior and testable work, not
unreviewed Rust signatures or a predetermined hierarchy of tiny modules.

## Ordered phases and chunks

Every Cxx has its own instruction file under [chunks](chunks/README.md). Complete
chunks in numeric order. A dependency delay blocks its chunk; report it honestly
and continue only research/preparation explicitly within that chunk. Do not
silently advance a later phase or replace a real dependency with a fake to pass.
For a prolonged upstream stall, report the exact dependency and offer an owner
reorder/scope decision. Do not wait indefinitely or reorder silently; an approved
change updates predecessors, plan and review evidence before execution.

